About
=====
This small tool will install an event hook into the Sirion USB stack and print out IO
tracing information to the console. Nothing more, nothing less.

Once started press CTRL+C to stop the tool again.

Command arguments
-----------------
usbiotrace takes an optional set of arguments used to form a filter for the IO trace
events to capture. The filtering argument will be AND'ed together, meaning that only
trace events matching all of the filter arguments specified will be captured.
If a filter argument is not specified it defaults to matching anything, i.e. the
filter will not affect the captured events. In effect, not specifying any filter
arguments will trace all possible IO events.

BUSNUMBERS=BUSSES/K
 A string holding a comma sepparated list of USB bus numbers from which to
 trace IO events.
 The USB bus numbers can be found by inspecting the output of the
 "usbinfo tree" command for the HCDs to trace IO from.
 
FUNCTIONIDS=FKTIDS/K
 A string holding a comma separated list of UsbRawFunction identifiers for
 which to trace IO events.
 The UsbRawFunction identifiers can be found by inspecting the output of the
 "usbinfo tree" command for the USB Functions to trace IO from.

INTERFACEIDS=IFCIDS/K
 A string holding a comma separated list of UsbRawInterface identifiers for
 which to trace IO events.
 The UsbRawInterface identifiers can be found by inspecting the output of the
 "usbinfo tree" command for the USB Interfaces to trace IO from.

ENPOINTIDS=EPIDS/K
 A string holding a comma separated list of UsbEndPoint identifiers for
 which to trace IO events.
 The UsbEndPoint identifiers can be found by inspecting the output of the
 "usbinfo tree" command for the USB EndPoints to trace IO from.

ENDPOINTNUMBERS=EPNUMS/K
 A string holding a comma separated list of endpoint numbers for which to
 trace IO events. The endpoint numbers are the numbers found in the endpoint
 descriptor for a given endpoint. Each USB Function may contain an endpoint
 with a given endpoint number, so filtering by endpoint number can give
 traces across multiple USB Functions and USB busses.
 EndPoint numbers can be found by inspecting the output of the "usbinfo tree"
 command for USB EndPoints to trace IO from.

ENDPOINTTYPES=EPTYPES/K
 A string holding a comma separated list of endpoint types for which to
 trace IO events. The possible endpoint type values are

  - "Control" / "Ctrl"
    Includes Control endpoints in the filter

  - "Isochronous" / "Iso"
    Includes Isochronous endpoints in the filter

  - "Bulk"
    Includes Bulk endpoints in the filter

  - "Interrupt" / "Int"
    Include Interrupt endpoints in the filter

IOCOMMANDS=IOCMDS/K
 A string holding a comma separated list of IO commands for which to
 trace IO events. The possible IO command values are
 
  - "CMD_READ" / "READ"
    Include CMD_READ IO. I.e. data reads from USB endpoints
  
  - "CMD_WRITE" / "WRITE"
    Include CMD_WRITE IO. I.e. data writes to USB endpoints

PORTPATH=PATH/K
 A string holding the path to a specific USB Function or topology subtree
 for which IO is to be traced. You can either construct this port path
 from knowledge of the USB topology you want to trace, or by using the
 usbinfo command and inspect the port path information of the "Hub"
 property of the Function you want to trace IO to.

 The value is a string of the format
 
   [PathRootFunctionId[:]]PortPath[/*]
   
 where
  - PathRootFunctionId is the UsbRawFunction identifier of the hub from
    which the actual port path given will be reckoned.
    If specified along with a port path, the PathRootFunctionId and the
    port path must be separated with a colon.
    If omitted, the root of the port path is the HCD root port.

  - PortPath a path of hub ports leading down the USB topology to the USB
    Function at (and possibly below) which IO tracing may occur.
    Each hub port specified is separated from the next by a slash. There
    is no trailing slash in a port path.
    A hub port may be specified as "?", meaning any hub port at this
    topology level matches the port path filtering.
    
  - If a trailing "/*" is specified in the port path (or just a "*" if
    specified directly after the PathRootFunctionId) it means that IO
    tracing should be enabled for the entire USB topology at, and below,
    the specified port path. If no "*" is specified in the path only an
    exact match will allow IO tracing - i.e. the port path is specific
    to an exact location in the USB topology.

 For example

 - "PATH=0x0452BDE0" will trace IO for any Function which is connected
   directly to a port on hub Function 0x0452BDE0.

 - "PATH=0x0452BDE0/*" will trace IO for any Function which is connected
   anywhere in the USB topology below the hub Function 0x0452BDE0.

 - "PATH=0x0452BDE0:2/1" will trace IO only for the Function which is
   connected directly to port 1 on the hub which is connected to port 2
   on hub Function 0x0452BDE0.

 - "PATH=0x0452BDE0:2/1/*" will trace IO for any Function which is connected
   anywhere in the USB topology at, or below, port 1 on the hub which is
   connected to port 2 on hub Function 0x0452BDE0.

 - "PATH=0x0452BDE0:?/1" will trace IO only for the Function which is
   connected directly to port 1 on a hub which is connected to any port
   on hub Function 0x0452BDE0.

TRACEPOINT=POINT/K
 A string holding a comma separated list of trace points for which to
 trace IO events. The possible trace point values are
 
 - "BEGIN"
   Capture IO request when it reaches usbsys.device from its originator.

 - "END"
   Capture IO request when usbsys.device is replying it back to the originator.

 - "HCDBEGIN"
   Capture IO request when a HCD traces it as received.

 - "HCDXFER"
   Capture IO progress when a HCD traces that a data transfer has been
   performed on the IO request.

 - "HCDCUSTOM"
   Capture any custom data a HCD may trace

 - "HCDEND"
   Capture IO request when a HCD traces it as being replied

 Notice that all the "HCD#?" trace points depend on the HCDs partaking in
 IO tracing. It is optional for a HCD to support IO tracing.

TRACEDATA=DATA/S
 A switch argument which, if present, will make IO tracing capture the
 data buffer content of an IO request as it is being traced.
 For some IO trace events the data buffers may be unavailable to avoid
 interfering with e.g. DMA data transfers. In such a case no data buffer
 capture will take place.
